<!--
  ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   NASHAT TV — STRINGS (English, source of truth)
  ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

   Every user-visible word in the app lives here and is translated into
   Kurdish Sorani (values-ckb) and Arabic (values-ar). Both translations
   are COMPLETE — the previous build was missing 52 keys in each, including
   every string on the Live TV screen, so a Kurdish user opened the app to
   an English home screen.

   VOICE
   · Say what happened and what to do next. "Couldn't load channels. Check
     your connection and try again." — not "Error loading content".
   · Second person, present tense, no exclamation marks.
   · Sentence case for everything except the LIVE badge. Title Case makes
     an interface feel like a form.
   · No emoji in strings. An emoji is an image; images belong in drawables,
     where they can be tinted, sized, and swapped per theme. The old copy
     shipped "⭐ Rate App" and "🔥 Featured" as literal text.
   · Never name a technology the user did not choose. "Couldn't reach the
     server" — not "Firebase error" or "TMDB request failed".

   TRANSLATION NOTES
   · Kurdish and Arabic run 20–40% longer than English. Nothing here is
     written to fit a fixed width; layouts wrap.
   · %1$s style positional arguments everywhere, never bare %s, because
     Kurdish and Arabic word order moves the substitution point.
   · translatable="false" marks endpoints and identifiers, so translators
     are not shown a URL to localise.
-->
